@node introduction "Introduction"

@{b}INTRODUCTION@{ub}

@{b}What is ApolloVNC (anyway:)?@{ub}

ApolloVNC is a fast VNC client specifically tailored to the Vampire. This means that it uses specific features of the Vampire cards (PIP, AMMX, YUV) to make it the fastest VNC solution available at the time of writing. In addition it supports some really cool features like bidirectional clipboard and the possibility to download, convert and play videos or audio files directly via RiVA or an audio player.

@{b}How does ApolloVNC compare to "classic" VNC?@{ub}
ApolloVNC is not a full VNC suite, it relies on other programs to create a server-client environment that guarantees connecting a Linux server to a Vampire client. The most important are:

@{b}Linux:@{ub}
- x11vnc: a "classic" Linux VNC server that will handle mouse and keyboards events only ("headless setup")
- avs: ApolloVNCServer program that converts, compresses and transmits framebuffer data to the Vampire client (avc)

@{b}Vampire:@{ub}
- zvnc4avnc: Zero VNC for ApolloVNC is an adapted version of ZVNC that collects and transmits mouse and keyboard events from the Vampire to the x11vnc server on Linux ("headless setup")
- avc: ApolloVNCClient program that receives, decompresses and shows screenbuffer data on the Vampire.

@{b}How do the clipboard and video features work?@{ub}
When used with clipboard and video download features, ApolloVNC relies on additional tools to work correctly. To name just the most important: smbd (SAMBA for data exchange), xclip (clipboard functionalities), yt-dlp (video download), ffmpeg (video conversion).

@{b}On what systems does ApolloVNC run?@{ub}
ApolloVNC runs both on classic AmigaOS and ApolloOS. A V4 card with a recent core (>11403) is strongly recommended, but V2 is also supported (core 2.18 required, PIP and YUV are replaced by SoftPIP and RGB color modes, also V2 has to use an older version of RiVA which cannot perform at the same level as later versions on V4). Rudimentary support (= mainly for debugging purposes and not officiallz maintained at the moment) is provided for UAE (RGB8 Softpip color mode).

Another short description of ApolloVNC on the Apollo Vampire Lair:

@{" Apollo Vampire Lair " link "apollovampirelair"}  

@{" Back to TOC " link "main"}

@endnode

@node apollovampirelair "Apollo Vampire Lair"

@{b}ApolloVNC on Apollo Vampire Lair@{ub}

Did you ever want to use a @{b}modern Webbrowser@{ub} (Firefox, Chromium (..)), a @{ub}fully featured office suite@{ub} (LibreOffice, Calc, Impress) or a @{b}Photoshop@{ub} tool like GIMP directly from your Vampire - and in addition @{b}easily exchange@{ub} data between the two via @{b}SAMBA@{ub} and @{b}clipboard@{ub}? Have you ever dreamed of watching any @{b}Youtube@{ub} (or other) video natively on your Vampire at @{b}full framerate@{ub} and up to 848x480 resolution? Or listen to a Youtube @{b}soundtrack@{ub}? Would you like to get an easily configurable @{b}WiFi connection@{ub} for your Vampire?

Then ApolloVNC is the tool you should use!

ApolloVNC is a fast (the @{b}fastest@{ub}) @{b}VNC@{ub} server-client solution available for your Vampire! It requires a @{b}Linux@{ub} box (@{b}Debian/Ubuntu MATE@{ub} recommended and officially supported) running on a fast (ideally > 2 Ghz, the faster the better) @{b}x86-64@{ub} processor. ApolloVNC uses Vampire hardware accelerations (AMMX, PIP, YUV) to get transfer rates up to 25 FPS (>40 fps peek performance in YUV2N mode). Thanks to @{b}automated installers@{ub} both on Linux and Vampire, ApolloVNC is @{b}easy@{ub} to install and works out-of-the-box. Thanks to PIP (= windowed mode) ApolloVNC integrates nicely into your AmigaOS / ApolloOS Workbench. Alternatively, it can be run in fullscreen or dual mode (which allows you use classic screen switching to go forth and back between windowed and fullscreen mode).

Thanks to its @{b}bidirectional clipboard@{ub}, ApolloVNC makes it easy to copy text from and to programs running on Linux and AmigaOS/ApolloOS. The same is true for video playback: ApolloVNC contains the latest version of @{b}RiVA@{ub} which is automatically installed and configured. Youtube (and other) videos can then be played (and also stored and replayed) by @{b}a simple mouse click@{ub}!

Using @{b}ApolloWoE@{ub} (= WiFi-over-Ethernet) you will not only get an even faster connection to your VNC server, but can also connect your Vampire to the internet via the Linux-WiFi.

Try it out! We truly believe that ApolloVNC is a game changer that gives the ultimate possibility to use your Vampire for anything you want - even as a daily driver with access to any modern service you can think of!

Link: @{" The Vampire Lair " system "OpenURL https://www.apollo-vampire-lair.com"} 

@{" Back to TOC " link "main"}

@endnode

@node requirements "Requirements"

@{b}Requirements@{ub}

@{b}LINUX@{ub} 
- Ubuntu Mate [24.04.3 or 24.04.4 LTS] / Debian Mate [13.4 Trixie] are the officially supported distributions and desktops (i.e. automatic installers are tested and proven to work) (*) (**)
- These distributions contain all additional packages needed (x11vnc, xclip, ffmpeg, yt-dlp).
- ApolloVNC must be run on a x86-64 little-endian machine in 32bit color mode (anything else is NOT supported).
- The Linux desktop must be set to a SAGA resolution or a resolution lower than the actual WB resolution for windowed mode (PIP).

(*) ApolloVNC has been successfully tested on other Linux distribution. Please make sure that if you use a different distribution X11 is used as display server (Wayland is NOT supported).
(**) @{b}IMPORTANT:@{ub} If you intend to use @{b}ApolloCrossDev@{ub} in conjunction with ApolloVNC you must use @{b}Ubuntu Mate 24.04.3 LTS@{ub}!
[ApolloCrossDev will NOT work on Ubuntu 25.10 and Debian 13.2. ApolloVNC, on the other hand, has been tested to work well on these versions.]
[@{b}UPDATE 202601:@{ub} ApolloCrossDev may now work on Debian (and maybe other distributions) - but this has not yet been fully tested, so Ubuntu remains the official recommendation.]


@{b}VAMPIRE@{ub}

@{b}V4@{ub}
- Core >11403 required (faster, support for native YUV4 and 720x405 videos)
- RiVA 0.63 or later for video playback

@{b}V2@{ub}
- Core 2.18 required
- PIP replaced by SoftPIP (slower)
- YUV replaced by RGB (slower and less accurate)
- RiVA 0.63 replaced by RiVA 0.54 (slower)

IMPORTANT

ApolloVNC may FREEZE on older cores (especially V2) because it requires features / instructions that are NOT present on earlier cores. This version should work on the latest release cores (11350, 12xxx) and probably also older V4 cores (preferably >10000, not tested extensively). In any case, we STRONGLY recommend to update to the latest core!

@{b}OPERATING SYSTEMS@{ub}

ApolloVNC has been tested on ApolloOS (1), AmigaOS 3.1, AmigaOS 3.9 (Coffin), AmigaOS 3.2, Amikit XE.

@{b}NETWORK STACKS@{ub}

ApolloVNC has been tested with AROSTCP, AmiTCP, MiamiDX and Roadshow. (The fastest and redommended network stacks are AROSTCP and Roadshow, but the others work too.)

(1) Due to still unresolved incompatibilities with ApolloOS HIDD screens, FULLSCREEN option will not work correctly on ApolloOS 9.5 (use PIP instead).

@{" Back to TOC " link "main"}

@endnode

@node glossary "Glossary"

@{b}Glossary@{ub}

@{b}avc@{ub}
ApolloVNCClient is the Amiga program that receives, decompresses and shows screendata from avs.

@{b}avs@{ub} 
ApolloVNCServer is the Linux program that reads, compresses and sends screendata to avc. 

@{b}AVNC@{ub}
Short for ApolloVNC (refers to the entire ApolloVNC project, client and server). The volume name AVNC: is used for the SAMBA share that allows exchanging data between Linux (server) and AmigaOS (client).

@{b}e4v@{ub}
Extract for Vampire is a bash script (Linux) which downloads and converts videos calling yt-dlp.

@{b}WoE@{ub}
Wifi-over-Ethernet allows you to connect your Vampire to your Linux box via an Ethernet cable and share the WiFi connection.

@{b}x11vnc@{ub}
This is the Linux program that handles keyboard and mouse inputs coming from zvnc4avnc and sends it to X11 display server.

@{b}yt-dlp@{ub}
YouTube DownLoad Program is a Linux program originally developped to download Youtube videos from the command line. In the meantime it can download videos from many other sites.

@{b}zvnc4avnc@{ub}
ZVNC for AVNC. Reads keyboard and mouse events on AmigaOS and sends them to x11vnc (Linux).

@{b}ZVNC@{ub}
ZeroVNC is a "headless VNC" program (= no screen data is transferred) originally developpend by David Gerber for MorphOS. ZVNC only transmits keyboard and mouse events to the X11 server (x11vnc). ZVNC has been adapted (and named zvnc4avnc) to work with ApolloVNC screens (SAGA) and windows (PIP).

@{" Back to TOC " link "main"}

@endnode

@node versions "Versions"

@{b}Versions@{ub}

@{b}DEMO / FULL VERSION@{ub}
ApolloVNC comes either as DEMO or FULL VERSION:
- LE = Limited Edition = DEMO: The use of this version is limited to 5 minutes (available via purelab-tefc.ch/vampire).
- FE = Full Edition: This version has no time limit (available via membership at apollo-vampire-lair.com).

Executable:
- XE = Hybrid Edition: The same exe is used for FE/LE (FE without license defaults to LE).

Each version is tagged in the following way:
- A = Alpha version (FE, not for public testing, license expires after 1 month)
- B = Beta version (FE, public testing, license expires after 1 month)
- R = Release version (FE/LE, tested official version)
- F = Final version (legacy compile with SASC, no further development, eliminated)

Each version can be clearly identified by a version string which also contains the year, month and release number of the version.

@{b}26.05 BETA@{ub}

Update with the following features:
- New SAGA Pointer handling (smarthide, core >12000)
- Dual-PIP support / parallel video playback (core >11900)
- New YUV2N/YUV3N PIP color encodings (very fast, core >11403)
- Optional luma/chroma correction (better contrasts YUV2N/YUV3N)
- Hacky VBL handlers replaced by AddIntServer (ApolloVNC & RiVA)
- Updated release version for RiVA (0.63 R3)
- New tooltypes (task priority, parallel video)
- Added TCP_QUICKACK for lower latency (ApolloVNCServer)
- ApolloWoE config & launcher (Wifi for Vampire)
- License key and validation (same exe for LE/FE)
- Rudimentary support for m68k/UAE (RGB8/BGRA32/SOFTPIP)
- Added around a dozen options for ApolloVNCConnect MUI GUI 
- Added ApolloVNCConnect configuration to installer
- Bugfixes (clipboard size, YUV4N, CKEY, VIDEOPATH, FSDEPTHGADGET, 
  audiooptions, pointer smart/hide, memory leak AVS, yt-dlp etc.)
- Improved overall stability (no more DEADENDS - touch wood;)
- Included ApolloWheel installation (USB mouse wheel support)
- Optional installation of ApolloCrossDevGeany included
- Eliminated all legacy builds / versions

@{b}26.3 R1@{ub}

Update with the following features:
- Native YUV4 (cores >11403)
- RGB color encodings (16/12/9/8 bits)
- V2 support (FS/SoftPIP/Scaled, installer)
- Rewritten compression algorithm (C/ASM)
- Seamless remote mouse pointer handling
- Hiding / Smart hiding for AmigaOS pointer
- Automatic extraction / playback for MP3
- Parallel (asynchronous) video/audio playback(*)
- Optimizations (e.g. speed-up data transmission)
- New tooltypes / options (e.g. avg/max bitrates)
- Shared Memory (XShm) option for ApolloVNCServer
- Custom volume names (multiple SAMBA shares)
- New 68080 build system (SASC => ApolloCrossDev)
- Bugfixes (e.g. PIP, header size, video reencoding)
- ApolloVNCConnect by TuKo (GUI for quick connections)
- Desktop launcher for ApolloVNCServer (start / stop)
[- WIP ApolloVNCServer for AmigaOS / ApolloOS]
(*) when technically possible (e.g. with SoftPIP)

@{b}26.2 F1@{ub}

This is a legacy version (= the last one compiled with SASC) included in the 26.3 release (inside SASC folder) to guarantee some backwards compatibility on V4. It runs on the last release V4 release cores (11350) and maybe even earlier cores (not tested). This version will NOT work on V2 cores <=2.17 (core 2.18 is mandatory for V2).

@{b}25.12 R1@{ub}

Update with the following features:
- Bidrectional clipboard support (text)
- Video download (yt-dld) and conversion (ffmpeg)
- Native video playback via RiVA (SAMBA)
- Scaling function with smooth color filtering
- Improved keyboard support for special characters
- Numerous new tooltypes and configuration options
- Automatic install scripts (Ubuntu / Debian Mate)
- Direct launch of ApolloVNCServer at startup (optional autologin)
- Bugfixes (stability and speed)

@{b}25.10 R1@{ub}

Initial version with the following main features:
- FULLSCREEN, PIP and DUAL mode
- Variable color compression (from YUV4 to YUV8)
- Fast AMMX blitting and PIP hardware acceleration
- Separate Y/U/V Run-length encoding (RLE) for data compression
- Native AmigaOS mouse pointer for maximum reactivity
- Password and IP restriction support
- Full custom screen and PIP handling via VBL interrupt
- Clean disconnect / reconnect (error) handling
- Direct launch of a specific program on the server
- Many options to configure / adapt the program
- Detailed user guide in AmigaGuide format

@{" Back to TOC " link "main"}

@endnode

@node installation "Installation"

@{b}SETTING UP ApolloVNC@{ub}

Installing ApolloVNC used to be tricky and challenging in the past. But here's the good news: Thanks to an automated install this is now as simple as running two scripts (one on Linux and one on the Vampire). This means that ANYONE now should be able to make a working installation of ApolloVNC in 30-60 mins (installing one of the Linux distributions included).

@{b}(1) PRELIMINARY STEPS@{ub}

@{b}(a) INSTALL LINUX@{ub}
- Download latest Ubuntu 24.04 LTS Mate oder Debian (e.g. netinstaller) as iso or dvd image
- Flash the image to a USB stick
- Start you computer pressing ESC or DEL key to boot from the USB stick (depends on the compuer / BIOS you use)
- Follow the instructions of the installer (on Debian deselect gnome desktop and install Mate instead)

Tips and tricks:
- You can chose (almost) any Linux username and any password you want ...
BUT:
(1) You MUST use a password has NOT MORE THAN 8 characters (this is a limitation of x11vnc)
(2) You must use THIS SAME USERNAME and PASSWORD later in ALL PARTS of the installer (e.g. also for x11vnc and SAMBA)
- Example: username=vncuser, password=12345678
- On Debian, use the same password for user and root
(Debian does not setup sudo by default.)
PLEASE NOTE THAT LINUX IS CASE-SENSITIVE (so, 'vncuser' is NOT the same as 'VNCuser').

Debian:
- Deselect Gnome desktop and install Mate instead.

Ubuntu:
- You are given the possibility to chose with or without login (see 'Running ApolloVNC' for more details).
- It is recommended to install 3rd party components and multimedia extensions.

@{b}(b) DOWNLOAD APOLLOVNC@{ub}
- You can get the FE = full version at apollo-vampire-lair.com (otherwise use the free LE = demo version which is limited to 5 minutes).
- Extract the LHA file (e.g. on AmigaOS) and copy it entirely both to Linux (e.g. home directory) and the Vampire

HINT:
It is possible to extract the LHA file directly on Debian/Ubuntu by installing the lhasa package:
sudo apt-get install lhasa

=> Debian does not support sudo out-of-the box, so use the graphical installer (in "Control Center") instead and search for "lhasa" package.

@{b}(2) INSTALLATION@{ub}

@{b}(a) LINUX@{ub}
@{b}(1)@{ub} 
Open a shell / terminal and go to ApolloVNC folder (adapt the command depending on where you extracted the lha-archive):
cd ApolloVNC_FE/Linux
[e.g. if your archive was extracted to the Downloads folder, you must type: cd Downloads/ApolloVNC_FE/Linux instead and use the TAB for autocomplection.]

@{b}(2)@{ub}
@{b}(a)@{ub} If you are on Debian, first run the make-sudo script changing to the Linux folder and then typing (the dot . at the beginning is important):
. make-sudo
[You will have to do a complete LOGOUT / LOGIN to make the changes take effect]
@{b}(b)@{ub} Now run the install script (the dot . at the beginning is important):
cd ApolloVNC/Linux
. install-avnc

@{b}IMPORTANT:@{ub}
The script stops several times and asks for a password (for root access, x11vnc, SAMBA etc.):
- You must enter the EXACT SAME PASSWORD that you used for the username during the Linux install!
(The default passwords - see above - are proposed.)
- Confirm ALL actions by typing 'y' or 'Y' as asked by the script.

=> If anything fails (e.g. you typed wrong one of the passwords), rerun the script.

@{b}(b) AMIGA / VAMPIRE@{ub}
- Open a shell and change to ApolloVNC folder (= folder that contains ApolloVNCClient)
- Run the installer by typing:
ApolloVNCClientInstall 192.168.1.108 vncuser 12345678

NOTE:
It is possible to add a custom volume name as 4th argument (otherwise "avnc" is used as default;  do not use : here, it will automatically be added by the smb-handler).
If you want to use all functionalities (e.g. including ApolloVNCConnect GUI) please answer "y" (yes) to the questions of the installer.

IMPORTANT:
The installer needs 3 (or 4) arguments like shown in the example. Replace these by YOUR personal and settings - use the EXACT SAME SETTING as on Linux.
- Reboot your Vampire and establish a network connection
- Configuration of WoE (WiFi-over-Ethernet) is launched automatically by the installer (skip it if you don't want to use it).

That's all - now proceed to "Running ApolloVNC".

@{" Back to TOC " link "main"}

@endnode

@node running "running the programs"


@{b}RUNNING APOLLOVNC@{ub}

@{b}(a) LINUX@{ub}

@{b}ICON@{ub}
The simplest way to start / stop ApolloVNCServer is to click on the two icons on the MATE main desktop. This corresponds to launching ApolloVNCServer with password and ip restriction (all clients in the subnet 255.255.255.x will be allowed to connect).

@{b}SHELL@{ub}
Another way to run avs is to launch it manually from a normal user shell (= not as ROOT):

cd /path/to/apollovncserver
./avs pw ip=192.168.1.0-255

This means that the server will be started using a password and an ip restriction (all IP addresses from 192.168.1.x are allowed to connect).

@{b}WoE@{ub}
If you want to use WiFi-over-Ethernet click on the ApolloWoE launcher make your WiFi accessible.
(If you did not configure it during install, run: ./WoE.sh install from the ApolloVNCServer folder.)

@{b}(b) AMIGA / VAMPIRE@{ub}
Click on the ApolloVNC icon and wait until the connection has been established.

=> The server may take some seconds to respond the first time it gets the request.
=> You can try to "wake it up" by pinging the server IP from the Vampire:
ping 192.168.1.x
[Replace it by your IP address.]

@{b}ApolloVNCConnect@{ub}
Another way to establish a connection between the ApolloVNCClient and the ApolloVNCServer is the GUI ApolloVNCConnect. Enter your ip, pw and a color mode, then hit "Connect" or "Save". Please note: This will only give you a simple VNC connection (screen + mouse/keyboard events - other functionalities like bidrectional clipboard and video/audio download/playback are not [yet] available via ApolloVNCConnect).

@{b}AUTOMATIC LAUNCH@{ub}
Launching ApolloVNCServer manually (which means clicking on the icon or opening a shell) can be a bit annoying. To launch ApolloVNCServer automatically when the user logs in do the following (the procedure is slightly different on Debian or Ubuntu, but the basic steps remain the same):

Debian:
On your Mate desktop click on menu "System" => "Preferences" => "Personal" => "Startup Applications". Once the window opens, click on "+Add", use "Browse" to find up the avs executable. Finally add the necessary parameters. Your command line (for example for user "vncuser") should look like this: /home/vncuser/ApolloVNC/Linux/avs pw ip=192.168.1.0-255

Ubuntu:
Click on "Menu" => "Control Center", scroll down to "Startup Applications" and use "Add" like explained for Debian.

IMPORTANT: 
You must have installed ApolloVNC with install-avnc before to make sure that all necessary paths and the home directory are set correctly.

@{b}AUTOLOGIN@{ub}
You can even go further and make the user login automatic (this way everything is started automatically, you only need to power on your Linux box and can directly connect to it from your Vampire once it has booted).

To do so, open the configuration file of the login manager (lightdm):
sudo pluma /etc/lightdm/lightdm.conf

Then change (= complete or uncomment) the following line:
autologin-user=vncuser

The user "vncuser" will automatically be logged in (and if autostart is selected, ApolloVNC will be started automatically).

PLEASE NOTE THAT ANY USER THAT CAN PHYSICALLY PRESS THE POWER BUTTON OF YOUR SYSTEM WILL HAVE ACCESS TO IT WITHOUT ENTERING A PASSWORD! (In general Ubuntu and Debian lock the screen after a certain time and you must then enter a password again - which can even be done via ApolloVNC - but your system is NOT protected at startup.)

There is the possibility to lock the screen immediately after AUTOLOGIN using the 'startup' option (please refer to the sections OPTIONS or FAQ for further information).

